te gastrointestinal obstruction in neonate"]}, "branches": {"B1": {"id": "B1", "label": "Congenital Structural Anomalies of the Gastrointestinal Tract", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.7542679772374549, "posterior": 0.39459782784606834, "danger": 0.8, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["Neonatal Down syndrome features (upslanting palpebral fissures, epicanthal folds, simian crease) elevate the suspicion for congenital structural GI anomalies l…"], "evidence_against": ["The delayed but successful rectal evacuation argues against complete structural obstruction (like duodenal atresia), though structural anomalies remain the lea…"], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B1.1", "B1.2", "B1.3", "B1.4"], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.6, "interrupt_relevance": 0.7, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "anatomy"}, "B2": {"id": "B2", "label": "Obstruction due to Meconium Accumulation / Plug Syndrome", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "live", "prior": 0.13967925504397313, "posterior": 0.06959386332370905, "danger": 0.7, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": ["Absence of air in meconium on radiograph supports meconium plug syndrome over meconium ileus, particularly in a neonate with Down syndrome who typically presen…"], "evidence_against": ["Passage of first stool after 48 hours in a Down syndrome neonate reduces likelihood of meconium plug syndrome (usually <24 hours), strengthening suspicion for …"], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.4, "interrupt_relevance": 0.5, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "etiology"}, "B3": {"id": "B3", "label": "Functional Intestinal Obstruction in Developmental Disorders", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "live", "prior": 0.06466632177961718, "posterior": 0.04027422646047976, "danger": 0.75, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": ["Passage of first stool after 48 hours in a Down syndrome neonate reduces likelihood of meconium plug syndrome (usually <24 hours), strengthening suspicion for …"], "evidence_against": ["The ability to pass stool via rectal exam contradicts the classic presentation of Hirschsprung disease (complete obstruction), though Down syndrome's risk fact…"], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.3, "interrupt_relevance": 0.6, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "risk_context"}, "B4": {"id": "B4", "label": "Congenital Extra-Intestinal Anomalies with GI Complications", "level": 1, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.026, "danger": 0.6, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}, "B1.1": {"id": "B1.1", "label": "Duodenal Atresia", "parent": "B1", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.3519917227108123, "posterior": 0.1753765355757468, "danger": 0.8, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": ["Down syndrome’s 2-4% prevalence of duodenal atresia weakly supports this structural anomaly."], "evidence_against": ["Successful rectal evacuation conflicts with immediate complete obstruction typical of duodenal atresia but sparing Down syndrome’s overall structural risk."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.8, "interrupt_relevance": 0.75, "level_role": "specific_disease", "classification_axis": "anatomy"}, "B1.2": {"id": "B1.2", "label": "Intestinal Malrotation with Midgut Volvulus", "parent": "B1", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.301707190894982, "posterior": 0.18790343097401443, "danger": 0.8, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": ["Delayed stool evacuation aligns with variable obstruction patterns in Down syndrome, moderately supporting malrotation."], "evidence_against": ["Bilious vomiting with eventual decompression moderately weakens volvulus suspicion due to atypical acute presentation."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.8, "interrupt_relevance": 0.7, "level_role": "specific_disease", "classification_axis": "anatomy"}, "B1.3": {"id": "B1.3", "label": "Meconium Ileus", "level": 2, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.031, "danger": 0.7, "parent": "B1", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Down syndrome’s lower incidence of CFTR-related meconium ileus, combined with delayed stool passage, moderately argues against this diagnosis."]}, "B1.4": {"id": "B1.4", "label": "Hypertrophic Pyloric Stenosis in Neonates", "level": 2, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.0, "danger": 0.6, "parent": "B1", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}}, "frontier": ["B1.1", "B1.2", "B1.1", "B1.2", "B2", "B3"], "other_mass": 0.0, "pending_results": [], "actions_taken": [{"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "What evidence is MOST INCONSISTENT with Congenital Structural Anomalies of the Gastrointestinal Tract? Identify the single strongest finding that argues against this diagnosis.", "summary": "The delayed but successful rectal evacuation argues against complete structural obstruction (like duodenal atresia), though structural anomalies remain the leading diagnosis due to Down syndrome's known associations."}, {"t": 3, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the presence of Down syndrome-associated physical features (upslanting palpebral fissures, epicanthal folds, single transverse palmar crease) argue for duodenal atresia via the known 2-4% preval…", "summary": "Down syndrome’s 2-4% prevalence of duodenal atresia weakly supports this structural anomaly."}, {"t": 3, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the neonate’s 48-hour delay in spontaneous stool passage align with intestinal malrotation’s variable obstruction pattern (especially in Down syndrome patients with higher malrotation prevalence…", "summary": "Delayed stool evacuation aligns with variable obstruction patterns in Down syndrome, moderately supporting malrotation."}, {"t": 3, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the successful rectal evacuation of stool after 48 hours contradict the classic presentation of duodenal atresia (typically immediate complete obstruction), despite Down syndrome associations?", "summary": "Successful rectal evacuation conflicts with immediate complete obstruction typical of duodenal atresia but sparing Down syndrome’s overall structural risk."}, {"t": 3, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Do the early bilious vomiting and eventual rectal decompression make midgut volvulus less likely compared to more proximal obstructions, given volvulus typically presents with sudden severe obstructi…", "summary": "Bilious vomiting with eventual decompression moderately weakens volvulus suspicion due to atypical acute presentation."}, {"t": 3, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the Down syndrome diagnosis (with trisomy 21) and absence of immediate (<24h) meconium passage strongly argue against meconium ileus (vs. plug syndrome) due to Down syndrome’s atypical presentat…", "summary": "Down syndrome’s lower incidence of CFTR-related meconium ileus, combined with delayed stool passage, moderately argues against this diagnosis."}], "differential_history": [{"top": [["B1", 0.628], ["B3", 0.194], ["B2", 0.116]]}, {"top": [["B1", 0.754], ["B1.1", 0.352], ["B1.2", 0.302]]}, {"top": [["B1", 0.395], ["B1.2", 0.188], ["B1.1", 0.175]]}], "deliberation": {}, "interrupt": {"active": false, "reason": "No signs of life-threatening instability (airway, respiratory, haemodynamic, or neurological compromise).", "required_actions": []}, "termination": {"ready_to_stop": false, "termination_type": "continue", "reason": "B1 (posterior=0.395) does not yet meet the 0.75 posterior threshold for confirmation. Dangerous alternatives B1.1 (duodenal atresia) and B1.2 (malrotation) with posteriors ≥0.175 remain active and unresolved. Tree depth is at 2/3 of the max limit."}, "turn_budget_used": 3, "estimated_remaining_value": 0.75, "max_turn_budget": 5, "latest_action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "diagnosis_readiness_score": 0.18790343097401443, "benchmark_output_ready": false, "static_question": "Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "static_options": [{"id": "A", "description": "Duodenal atresia"}, {"id": "B", "description": "Hirschsprung disease"}, {"id": "C", "description": "Intestinal malrotation"}, {"id": "D", "description": "Meconium ileus"}, {"id": "E", "description": "Pyloric stenosis"}], "seen_evidence_ids": [], "seen_evidence_phenotypes": "{'pica', 'meconium ileus', 'Neonatal intestinal obstruction', 'Abnormality of the gastrointestinal tract', 'Morphological abnormality of the gastrointestinal tract', 'ileus'}", "mode_policy": {"benchmark_purity": true, "allow_external_knowledge": true}, "answer_option_mapping": {}, "tool_use_log": [], "root_revision_needed": false, "max_tree_depth": 3, "reasoning_ledger": {"anchor": {"hypothesis": "B1", "t": 0, "posterior_at_anchor": 0.628}, "leader": {"branch_id": "B1.2", "label": "Intestinal Malrotation with Midgut Volvulus", "posterior": 0.188, "leading_since_t": 0, "n_revisions": 0}, "leader_evidence": {"confirming": 1, "disconfirming": 1, "last_disconfirming_digest": "Bilious vomiting with eventual decompression moderately weakens volvulus suspicion due to atypical acute presentation."}, "action_intents": [{"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 3, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 3, "intent": "confirm"}, {"t": 3, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 3, "intent": "refute"}, {"t": 3, "intent": "broaden"}], "considered_alternatives": ["B1.1", "B2"]}, "discriminator_hints": "[Knowledge Layer: coverage=60%, source=primekg]\n\nDuodenal Atresia vs Intestinal Malrotation with Midgut Volvulus:\n  Favours first:  abnormal duodenum morphology, abnormality of the pancreas, abnormality of the pulmonary artery, annular pancreas, autosomal recessive inheritance\n  Favours second: abdominal distention, autosomal dominant inheritance, frontal bossing, high forehead, intestinal malrotation\n\nDuodenal Atresia vs Meconium Ileus:\n  Favours first:  abnormal duodenum morphology, abnormality of the pancreas, abnormality of the pulmonary artery, annular pancreas, autosomal recessive inheritance\n  Favours second: 2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal obesity, abnormal cardiac septum morphology, abnormal cardiac ventricular function, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology\n\nIntestinal Malrotation with Midgut Volvulus vs Meconium Ileus:\n  Favours first:  abdominal distention, autosomal dominant inheritance, intestinal malrotation, neonatal intestinal obstruction, telecanthus\n  Favours second: 2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal obesity, abnormal cardiac septum morphology, abnormal cardiac ventricular function, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology\n\nRelated to Duodenal Atresia: intestinal atresia (disease), non-syndromic gastroduodenal malformation, non-syndromic intestinal malformation\n\nRelated to Intestinal Malrotation with Midgut Volvulus: familial intestinal malrotation-facial anomalies syndrome, mendelian disease\n\nRelated to Meconium Ileus: cystic fibrosis associated meconium ileus, ileus, intestinal obstruction in the newborn due to guanylate cyclase 2c deficiency\n\n[Indirect reasoning chains (PrimeKG 2-hop):]\n  He was born at 37 weeks of gestation to a 38-year-old G3P3 woman → increased body weight → Meconium Ileus\n  The pregnancy was uncomplicated and the patient’s mother refused all prenatal testing → premature birth → Meconium Ileus\n  The pregnancy was uncomplicated and the patient’s mother refused all prenatal testing → seizure → Meconium Ileus"}
